The present study focused on the integration of beta-cyclodextrin based metal-organic frameworks ( $\dot{\boldsymbol{\beta}}$ -CDMOF) with polymer to obtain hybrid materials with advantageous properties compared to traditional single-component polymers or metal-organic frameworks (MOF) matrixes. We fabricated two complexes with different morphology and structure. During the in situ growth of $\beta$ -CDMOF around the hydrogel, potassium ions on polysaccharides gradually dissociated to participate in the growth of crystals, while other potassium ions on the carboxylic acid groups provided bridges between crystals and hydrogel, forming a necklace-shaped complex $(\mathsf{S H P s@}\beta$ -CDMOF). Hydrogen bonding and coordination interactions between $\beta$ -CDMOF and hydrogel are present in a dendritic sandwich-shaped complex $(\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@{\mathrm{SHPs}})$ . Furthermore, using the hydrophobic molecule curcumin as a model drug, we have demonstrated that $\displaystyle{S\mathrm{HPs}@\beta}$ -CDMOF and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF@SHPs}}$ hybrid materials stabilize the included drug and have potential for controlled drug release. Collectively, the integration of MOF with polymer holds a great promise for drug delivery applications.
## 1. Introduction
In 1967, Pedersen accidentally discovered that synthetic macrocyclic polyethers could form structurally stable complexes with alkali metal or alkaline earth metal cations, marking the birth of supramolecular chemistry (Pedersen, 1967). While macrocyclic polyethers were found to bind alkali metal cations stably, these hosts do not easily form scal­ able superstructure complexes like those of coordination polymers or metal organic frameworks (MOF) with Group IA or IIA metal cations (Beach & Plaut, 1970; Cram & Cram, 1983; Long & Yaghi, 2009). Nevertheless, chemists tried their best to construct well-defined supra­ molecular complexes, and Pedersen (1988), Cram (1988) and Lehn (1988) were awarded the 1987 Nobel Prize for their pioneering work in this field. Since all of these new non-covalent systems are based on weak interactions, such as hydrogen bonding, metal coordination, hydro­ phobic or hydrophilic forces, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions and van der Waals forces (Delbiancco et al., 2015; Rodell et al., 2015; Yang et al., 2015), chemists have focused on the study of coordination chemistry and host-guest chemistry for the construction of highly-ordered structures (Moha­ madhoseini & Mohamadnia, 2021).
Natural molecules, such as proteins, oligonucleotides, lipids and their multimolecular complexes, have been the major source of inspi­ ration for chemists (Uhlenheuer et al., 2010). Cyclodextrins (CDs) are among the most important molecular receptors studied in supramolec­ ular and coordination chemistry (Villaonga et al., 2007). Moreover, CDs can be regarded as ideal host frameworks, with characteristics that have enabled their use as enzyme models. The ability of different CDs to encapsulate a range of guests to form inclusion complexes has been exploited for sensing and drug delivery, in the food and cosmetics in­ dustries and other applications (Delbiancco et al., 2015; Cai et al., 2019). The U.S. Food and Drug Administration has classified CDs as “generally recognized as safe” (GRAS) and allowed their use in a variety of foods, cosmetics and pharmaceuticals (GRAS Notice No. GRN 000046; 000074; 000155). Recently, a series of metal-organic frameworks (MOF) was synthesized from CDs and Group IA metal cations (CD-MOFs), in which MOF was renewable and biocompatible (Forgan et al., 2012; Li et al., 2017; Smaldone et al., 2010; Volkova et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2021), because the $\upalpha$ -1,4 linked D-glucopyranosyl residues have a unique and prominent role in the formation of an extended structure with Group IA metal cations, and the presence of repeated –OCCO– binding motifs on both the primary and secondary faces of these cyclic oligosaccharides (Forgan et al., 2012). Among these CD-MOFs, $\beta$ -CDMOF has attracted the attention of researchers due to the following characteristics: 1) $\beta$ -CD is the most inexpensive and accessible of the CDs (Xu et al., 2019); 2) $\beta$ -CD is easier to combine with biomolecules via host-guest interactions (Jin et al., 2020); 3) $\beta$ -CD has multiple hydroxyl groups. In particular, the primary hydroxyl groups on the smaller ring are more nucleophilic than the secondary hydroxyls on the larger ring and can be used for functional modification (Zhan et al., 2018). Consequently, the chemistry and commercial potential of $\beta$ -CDMOF have been given more impor­ tance than those of similar CD-MOFs.
Although the applications of CD-MOFs have seen tremendous growth in the past two decades, studies have focused on MOFs in powder form and there are far fewer reports on the integration of MOFs into alter­ native forms (Howarth et al., 2016; Peterson et al., 2021). Polymers offer ideal media for the incorporation of MOF into films, binders, membranes and other forms (Koros & Zhang, 2017; Liu & Woll, 2017; Pastore & Cook, 2020; Rodenas et al., 2015). In general, there are two main ap­ proaches for the integration of polymers with MOFs: “MOF-first”, in which the pre-synthesized MOF is incorporated into the polymer by covalent or non-covalent interactions; or “polymer-first”, where the MOF is directly grown in or on the prepared polymer (Peterson et al., 2021). However, current researches on this type of polymer are mainly focused on fiber-based matrixes. The incorporation of MOFs with fibers usually requires modification of the surface of the fibers or MOFs to achieve matrix binding (Lin et al., 2018; Pastore & Cook, 2020). The modification process of the materials undoubtedly reduces the biosafety of the materials and increases the risk that may be posed (Giliopoulos et al., 2020). As natural plant polysaccharides, soybean hull poly­ saccharides (SHPs) bring a new perspective to broaden the use of MOFpolymer composites. In our previous work, the physicochemical prop­ erties of SHPs were explored (Liu et al., 2013; Wang, Zhao, et al., 2019a; Wang, Zhao, et al., 2019b), and various SHPs-based matrixes were developed for engineering applications (Wang, Shao, et al., 2019; Wang et al., 2020; Wang et al., 2021). Compared with conventional fiberbased matrixes, SHPs hydrogels have greater stability in a hard-acid environment $(\mathrm{pH}\sim4.0)$ . This provides a good alternative for the design of some special-purpose engineering applications. Herein, we report two forms of MOF-polymer composites, which were synthesized applying polymer-first and MOF-first strategies, respectively. In this work, the assembly mechanism of SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CDMOF is slightly different. During the synthesis of the necklace-shaped complex $\operatorname{\Lambda}(\operatorname{SHPs}(\mathcal{Q}\beta$ -CDMOF), the potassium ions bound to the hydroxyl group of hydrogel gradually dissociate and participate in the in-situ growth of $\beta$ -CDMOF, while the potassium ions located in the carboxylic acid group of hydrogel form a bridge between the crystals and hydrogel matrix. Coordination and hydrogen bonding are the main driving forces for the formation of dendritic sandwich-shaped complex $(\beta{\cdot}\mathrm{CDMOF}@\mathrm{SHPs})$ . We further explored the possibility of complexes as a model system for the delivery of hydrophobic curcumin (Moussa et al., 2016). The results demonstrate that $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF and $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs hybrid mate­ rials not only have advantages for preserving the structural integrity of the curcumin, but also have potential for controlling drug release.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
$\beta$ -CD $(M_{w}1134.55$ , $98+\%)$ , KCl, KOH, DMK, methanol, and ethanol were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Curcumin (Cur) was procured from Aladdin Chemistry Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Soybean hull polysaccharides (SHPs) were prepared according to our previous method (Liu et al., 2013), containing main kinds of monosaccharides such as galactose, xylose, galacturonic acid, arabinose, glucose, and rhamnose, and which SHPs was dialyzed to remove the excess salt (3500 D). The above soybean hull was kindly supplied by Shandong Yuwang Ecological Food Industry Co., Ltd. (Shandong, China). Ultra-pure (UP) water was used throughout the experiments. All reagents were commercially available and of pure analytical grade. Unless otherwise stated, the reagents were straightly used without further purification.
## 2.2. $\beta$ -CDMOF situ-growth on SHPs hydrogel (SHPs@β-CDMOF)
SHPs hydrogel were prepared by using the ionotropic gelation method described by Wang with some modification (Wang, Zhao, et al., 2019b). Briefly, SHPs $(0.5~\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{v}~\%)$ was dissolved in UP water and $^{1\mathrm{~M~}}$ KCl solution was slowly added dropwise through a syringe at a volume ratio of 2:1 for a while with gently stirred under ambient temperature. The resultant hydrogel was collected in the form of filtration or decan­ tation when which shape was fixed after being kept for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then, these hydrogel substrates were washed three times with ethanol solution (70 $\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}\mathbf{\Lambda}\stackrel{\circ\gamma}{\mathbf{\Lambda}}\mathbf{0}_{\rule{0ex}{5ex}}^{\bullet}$ to remove the excess potassium ions.
Furthermore, the amount of $\beta$ -CD $_{(\sim0.1~\ g)}$ was mixed with SHPs hydrogel in the form of $20~\mathrm{mL}$ solution with $5\mathrm{mL}$ methanol, and sealed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ beaker full of methanol vapor. After three days incubation at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , these colorless cubic MOF grew in situ in SHPs hydrogel substrates due to the reduced polarity of the mixed solution and the coordination of potassium ions. Meanwhile, the obtained hybrid materials were washed three times with $70\%$ ethanol and dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for overnight.
## 2.3. Post-coverage of $\beta$ -CDMOF with SHPs hydrogel (β-CDMOF@SHPs)
## 2.3.1. Synthesis of $\beta$ -CDMOF
In order to further explore the interaction of position between $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs hydrogel substrate on Cur loading and release behavior, the post-coverage of $\beta$ -CDMOF with SHPs hydrogel was con­ ducted by a solvent-thermal method.
$\beta$ -CDMOF was prepared from food grade components following the typical synthesis protocol described by our group (Wang et al., 2021). In brief, $\beta$ -CD (1.76 mmol, 2 g) was added in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ UP water solution containing potassium hydroxide (14.08 mmol, $0.79\:\mathrm{g})$ . Then the mixed solution was stirred at room temperature for $30\mathrm{min}$ under $500\mathrm{rpm}$ . The resulting solution was continuously stirred after added $\mathsf{5\ m L}$ methanol and filtered with a syringe filter membrane $(0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}})$ , sealed in a re­ action kettle containing methanol vapor, and which diffuse methanol vapor could be used as templates for $\beta$ -CDMOF preparation to promote the coordination bonding between organic ligand $({\beta}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CD})$ and linker metal (potassium ion). The subsequent $\beta$ -CDMOF crystals were prepared by dispersing $\beta$ -CD with 8.0 equiv. of KOH in aqueous solution, followed by MeOH vapor diffusing into the solution at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ during $12\mathrm{h}$ . After­ wards, these cubic crystalline were isolated from the solution, and washed three times with absolute ethanol to remove the extra unlinked potassium ions and organic ligand. Meanwhile, the colorless precipitates were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for overnight (yield about $80\%$ ).
Next, the collected $\beta$ -CDMOF crystals were dispersed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ ab­ solute ethanol and this turbid liquid was gently stirred. Then, $20\mathrm{mL}0.1$ wt% SHPs solution was slowly added dropwise through a syringe. The resulting mixed solution was placed in $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ condition under consistently stirred for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the complex of $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs hydrogel was decanted or filtered washed three times with ethanol (70 $\nu/{\bf{v}}\ \%)$ , the hybrid material was dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for overnight.
## 2.4. Release experiments
## 2.4.1. Drug loading
As a drug model, the hydrophobic molecule Cur was loaded in car­ riers following the typical impregnation protocol. Ten milligrams of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF and $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ SHPs were respectively immersed in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ $70\%$ ethanol solution with one milligrams of Cur for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , yielding drug inclusion compounds. The resulting compounds $(\mathsf{S H P s@}\beta\mathsf{-}$ - CDMOF@Cur and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@\operatorname{SHPs}@\operatorname{Cur})$ were washed thoroughly by solvent to remove unloaded Cur, and dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight under vacuum. For better comparison, the drug inclusion compounds of SHPs hydrogel $(\mathsf{S H P s@C u r})$ and $\beta$ -CDMOF crystal $(\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@\mathbf{Cur})$ were obtained through the same method. All of these carriers were treated by DMK before used in drug loading. The absorption peak of Cur in $70\%$ ethanol scanned in a range of $350\mathrm{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ by an UV–visible spectro­ photometer (UV-2500, Shimadzu, Japan) appeared at $430~\mathrm{nm}$ , and a standard curve of Cur concentration ranging from 1 to $12~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ was drawn; $y=0.1624x+0.0112$ , $R^{2}=0.9994$ . The encapsulation effi­ ciency (EE) and loading capacity (LC) were evaluated according to the Eqs. (1) and (2), respectively:
$$
\mathrm{EE}\left(\%\right)=\frac{M_{1}-M_{2}}{M_{1}}\times100\
$$
$$
L C\left(\%\right)=\frac{M_{1}-M_{2}}{T o t a l m a s s\ o f\ c a r r i e r}\times100\
$$
where, $M_{1}$ and $M_{2}$ are the initial and unencapsulated mass of Cur, and “Total mass of carrier” include the mass of carrier and encapsulated Cur, respectively.
## 2.4.2. Drug stability
Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) analysis was employed to characterize the thermal property of interaction between Cur and car­ riers by scanning during the first heating cycle at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}270^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $10\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}/$ min rate in a nitrogen atmosphere (Q2000, TA instrument, U.S.A.). After the baseline had been obtained by emptying the crucible in the first blank test, the sample $(\sim5\mathrm{mg})$ was placed in sealed alumina crucible for analysis. The fluorescence signal emitted from Cur in carriers was captured with a Horiba Jobin-Yvon fluorimeter equipped with a $100\mathrm{W}$ Xenon lamp and an R-928 detector operating at $950~\mathrm{V}$ , emission and excitation slits were both set at $5\mathrm{nm}$ (excited at $430\mathrm{nm}$ , monitoring the emission from $500\ \mathrm{nm}\ 750\ \mathrm{nm})$ ). UV–visible absorption spectropho­ tometer $350\mathrm{-}500\mathrm{nm})$ was also carried out to assess the stability of Cur. To focus on the interaction between carriers and Cur within the process of drug loading, FTIR were employed to record the wavenumber range from 4000 to $400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ .
## 2.4.3. Drug release in vitro
A sample of ten milligrams of Cur inclusion compounds redispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of release medium was placed into the dialysis bag (molecular cut off, 1000 D), and surrounded by $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ different medium $\mathrm{(pH}2.0$ , 4.0, 6.8, and 7.4 phosphate buffer) to respectively simulate the emptied stomach, fed stomach, colon, and small intestine (Bazban-Shotorbani et al., 2017). The amount of Cur released in simulated medium was recorded by UV–visible absorption spectrophotometer $\cdot430\ \mathrm{nm})$ at predetermined time points $(0{-}24~\mathrm{h})$ according to Eq. (3):
$$
(\mathcal{J}_{o})=\frac{V_{1}C_{C u r(n)}+V_{2}\sum C_{C u r(n-1)}}{M_{0}}\times100\
$$
where, $M_{0}$ represents the mass of Cur in carriers; $V_{1}$ and $V_{2}$ are the total volume of release medium and the withdrawn volume of release me­ dium at a given time; $C_{C u r(n)}$ and $C_{C u r(n-1)}$ are the concentration of Cur in release medium at $n$ times and $(n-1)$ times, respectively.
Several mathematical models of drug release kinetics in vitro were used to fit the release data, given by zero order (Eq. (4)), first order (Eq. (5)), Higuchi (Eq. (6)), and Korsmeyer-Peppas (Eq. $(7)$ ) (Abbas & Hanif, 2016). The $R^{2}$ value is commonly used to evaluate plots for all mathe­ matical functions thus fitted.
$$
{\begin{array}{l}{\mathbf{C}=k\times t}\\ {}\\ {C=100\times\left(1-e^{-k t}\right)}\\ {}\\ {C=k\times{\sqrt{t}}}\\ {C=k\times t^{n}}\end{array}}
$$
where $C$ is the concentration of released Cur at specified time; $k,t,$ and $n$ represent the dissolution constant, time, and release exponent, respectively.
## 2.5. Characterization
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Fabrication and characterization of samples
The $S_{\mathrm{{HPs}}\ @\beta}$ -CDMOF and $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ SHPs materials were prepared by following the above methods. The AFM images of SHPs (Fig. 1A), $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF (Fig. 1B), $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs (Fig. 1C), and $\beta$ -CDMOF (Fig. 1D) showed different structures and morphologies. SHPs were evenly distributed in the field of vision in a chain-like structure with a height of around $5\mathrm{nm}$ . By applying in situ growth protocols (Fig. 1E), the resultant $S_{\mathrm{MPs}@\beta}$ -CDMOF morphology was composed of $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs hydrogel. The MOF crystals were anchored to one side of the SHPs hydrogel and the height was around $10\mathrm{nm}$ . The surface of $\beta$ -CDMOF was covered with SHPs hydrogel by the post-synthesis protocol (Fig. 1F), giving $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@{\mathrm{SHPs}}$ with a height of around $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ . As shown in Fig. 1D, the height of $\beta$ -CDMOF was around $1\mathrm{nm}$ . These data indicated that materials composed of $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs were obtained despite the uneven distribution in the AFM images. Distinctive curves for $s_{\mathrm{HPs}}$ , $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF, $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF@SHPs}}$ , and $\beta$ -CDMOF were obtained in the thermodynamic analyses (Fig. 1G and H), illustrating the structural differences among the materials. Weight losses occurred between 230 and $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , demonstrating successful syntheses of $S_{\mathrm{MPs}@\beta}$ -CDMOF and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF@SHPs}}$ . The FTIR spectra (Fig. 1I) provided further evidence for the successful preparation of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF and $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs. The spectrum of $\beta$ -CDMOF contained a subtle broad absorption peak due to the C–O–C stretching vibration $(1020-1150\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) arising from crosslinking between units of 1,4-linked $\upalpha$ -D-glucopyranosyl residues from $\beta$ -CD in the $\beta$ -CDMOF (Smaldone et al., 2010). The spectrum of $\operatorname{SHPs}(\varnothing\beta\cdot$ - CDMOF contained a characteristic peak located at $3343\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ in the molecule. As is well known, there may be a weak interaction (hydrogen bonding or coordination effect) between hydroxyl groups present in two molecules, resulting in a shift of the infrared absorption peak position and a change of width. An addi­ tional peak appeared in the spectrum of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF at $1620\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to the COOH stretching vibration of saturated uronic acid in SHPs. In addition, compared with the SHPs spectrum, the COOH stretching vibration peak in the $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF spectrum was red-shifted. This indicated that there was a coordination interaction between $\beta$ -CDMOF crystals and SHPs from the in situ growth protocol. The infrared spec­ trum of $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs contained an absorption peak at $3377~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ . By comparing with the location of the hydroxyl group peaks in the infrared spectra of $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs $(3392~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\$ ), it was found that there was a hydrogen bonding interaction between SHPs and $\beta$ -CDMOF following the post-synthesis protocol, consistent with our previous research (Wang et al., 2021).
Further characterization of the materials obtained by in situ growth and post-synthesis protocols was conducted. The SEM images showed evenly dispersed $\beta$ -CDMOF as colorless cubes (Figs. 2A and S1A), consistent with the classic appearance of CD-MOF (Wu et al., 2013). The SEM images of $S_{\mathrm{{HPs}}\ @\beta}$ -CDMOF contained a necklace of MOF crystals (Figs. 2B and S1B), which was similar to the crystal growth of MOF-5 around fiber (Mirkovic et al., 2019). In contrast, $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs had a dendritic sandwich morphology (Figs. 2C and S1C). The goal of tunable arrangement between $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs hydrogel was ach­ ieved by the above protocols. In addition, the pore properties of the materials were analyzed using nitrogen physisorption at $77\ \mathrm{~K~}.$ All samples exhibited a type I isotherm with a characteristic sharp increase at low relative pressure (Fig. S2). The BET (Langmuir) surface areas of $\beta$ -CDMOF, $\displaystyle{\operatorname{SHPs}}\ @\beta$ -CDMOF, $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@\mathsf{S H P s}$ , and SHPs were esti­ mated to be 0.23, 0.49, 0.66 and $1.09\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively. The 2D layers in $\beta$ -CDMOF were stacked in a parallel staggered fashion, where va­ cancies in a layer were covered by $\beta$ -CD in adjacent layers, resulting in poor ${\bf N}_{2}$ absorption (Sha et al., 2015; Volkova et al., 2020). Additionally, XRD was used to confirm the crystal structures of the samples. Fig. 2D shows a typical diffractogram of $\beta$ -CDMOF crystals that was consistent with the simulated XRD data. Comparison of the characteristic diffrac­ tion peaks $(2\theta=6.38^{\circ}$ (101), $8.96^{\circ}$ (002), $10.60^{\circ}$ (110), $12.42^{\circ}$ (102)) indicated that SHPs hydrogel had no effect on the $\beta$ -CDMOF crystal structure in $S\mathrm{HPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF or $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ SHPs. These data suggested that ${\S}\mathrm{HPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF and $\beta$ -CDMOF $@S\mathrm{{HPs}}$ obtained by two different protocols were composed of both MOF crystals and hydrogel (Fig. 2E). Surface wettability is an essential physical property of MOF-polymer composites. Herein, $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF@SHPs}}$ complexes were characterized via a water contact angle (WCA) during 5 s. As shown in Fig. S3A, the WCA of $\beta$ -CDMOF was about $15^{\circ}$ in different pH media, which was similar to that of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF (Fig. S3B) due to in situ growth of crystals around the hydrogel. In $\mathrm{pH}2.0$ and $\mathrm{pH}4.0$ media, the WCA values of SHPs hydrogel were $25^{\circ}$ and $28^{\circ}$ (Fig. S3C), and those of $\beta$ -CDMOF $@s\mathrm{{HPs}}$ were $20^{\circ}$ and $24^{\circ}$ (Fig. S3D). The WCA values of both SHPs hydrogel and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF@SHPs}}$ were reduced at $\mathtt{p H}6.8$ and $\mathrm{pH}7.4$ , indicating pH-dependent interface wettability. The above data indicated that SHPs hydrogel exhibited higher hydrophobicity due to the gel structure density, which blocked penetration of the medium solution under hard-acid conditions $\mathrm{(pH~}2.0$ and 4.0). In addition, $\operatorname{SHPs}(\varnothing\beta.$ - CDMOF had a similar zeta potential to that of $\beta$ -CD-MOF, as did $\beta$ -CDMOF $@s\mathrm{{HPs}}$ and SHPs hydrogel (Fig. 2F), indicating that the spatial arrangement of $\beta$ -CDMOF in the SHPs hydrogel was different and that the co-assembly process did not rely on electrostatic interaction.
To unravel the mechanism of SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CDMOF assembly, Raman spectroscopy, which is complementary to IR spectroscopy, was used to monitor the interactions between $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs hydrogel, as shown in Fig. S4. The Raman data contained a distinct peak in the range of $3370{-}3140\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to $\scriptstyle{0-\mathrm{H}}$ stretching vibration of the samples, filtering out the effect of other interactions (e.g., coordination). In the spectrum of $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF@SHPs}}$ the broad peak around $3376~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was shifted to lower frequency compared with the $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs matrixes $(\sim3395\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ . This is probably due to an increased probability of contact and hydrogen bonding between SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CDMOF, which does not occur in $S_{\mathrm{MPs}@\beta}$ -CDMOF. To further elucidate the po­ tential mechanism of SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CDMOF assembly, $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra of samples dissolved in $\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}_{2}\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}$ were compared. The $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectra revealed the integration and chemical shift of signals corresponding to the anomeric proton of $\beta$ -CD in $\beta$ -CDMOF. The $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR data confirmed that SHPs (Fig. S5A) containing uronic acid could form complexes with MOF crystals. The ratios of $_{\beta\mathrm{-CD}}$ anomeric proton integration to the non-anomeric proton in $\displaystyle{\operatorname{SHPs}}\ @\beta$ -CDMOF (Fig. S5B) and $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ SHPs (Fig. S5C) were consistent with that of $\beta$ -CDMOF (1:6) (Fig. S5D), indicating that the SHPs hydrogel did not affect the crystal structure. In addition, the chemical shift of $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs showed that there was hydrogen bonding between SHPs and $\beta$ -CDMOF, but this was not observed in $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF.
Furthermore, XPS was carried out to elucidate the potential binding mechanism between SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CDMOF crystals (Wang et al., 2021). As illustrated in Fig. 3A, the N 1s signal assigned to the protein amino groups in SHPs was clearly visible at $399.7\mathrm{eV}$ in the full survey of all samples except $\beta$ -CDMOF, confirming the binding between SHPs and $\beta$ -CDMOF. Elemental analysis was also consistent with the above results (Table 1). In the optimized geometry, the electron localization function (ELF) showed significant electron-pair density among single-cell $\beta$ -CDMOF, supporting direct K–O bonding in the complexes (Fig. 3B). Furthermore, it was found that the peak in the O 1s spectrum $(0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{K})$ of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF was shifted by 0.35 eV toward higher energy compared to SHPs hydrogel in Fig. S6A, indicating an interaction be­ tween SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CD. This interaction caused an increase in O 1s binding energy during in situ growth of $\beta$ -CDMOF in the SHPs hydrogel due to the reduced charge shielding effect of oxygen bonded with potassium (Wang et al., 2021). Similarly, the O 1s peak in the $\beta$ -CDMOF $@s\mathrm{{HPs}}$ spectrum also increased by $0.42\mathrm{eV}$ compared to that of $\beta$ -CDMOF (Fig. S6A). In contrast, a decrease of a C 1s fit peak $(0{-}\mathsf{C}{=}0)$ in the spectrum of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF was caused by in situ growth of $\beta$ -CDMOF (Fig. S6B), but this shift was not seen in the spec­ trum of $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@{\mathrm{SHPs}}$ . These changes in binding energy were ascribed to interactions between potassium and $\beta\mathrm{-CD}$ as well as SHPs hydrogel. Consequently, there was a slight difference in the assembly mechanisms of SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CDMOF during the two protocols: 1) potassium ions bound to hydroxyl groups in the necklace $\operatorname{SHPs}(\varnothing\beta.$ -
CDMOF complex gradually dissociated and participated in the in situ growth of $\beta$ -CDMOF, while potassium ions located at the carboxylic acid groups formed the bridges between the crystal and hydrogel substrate (Fig. 3C); 2) binding forces (coordination and hydrogen bonding in­ teractions) are present in the dendritic sandwich $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@{\mathrm{SHPs}}$ complex obtained by covering the surface of $\beta$ -CDMOF crystals with SHPs (Fig. 3D).
## 3.2. Application in drug loading and release
Due to the sensitivity of the release medium to pH (Fig. S3), both $\operatorname{sHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF and $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs complexes may be suitable for drug delivery (Table S1). Hence, the hydrophobic drug, Cur, was chosen as a model to study the controlled release behavior of the complexes in simulated digestive fluid (Moussa et al., 2016). The visible light ab­ sorption spectrum of Cur solution is shown in Fig. 4A. A linear equation was obtained by fitting the standard curve of concentration and absor­ bance at $430~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 4B). Despite the low porosity of the complexes based on BET analysis, the loading of drug was possible. The values of EE and LC for $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF were $18.33\%$ and $1.78\%$ , respectively, and those for $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ SHPs were $15.13\%$ and $1.49\%$ (Fig. 4C and D). This is attributed to the ability of the MOF to increase the loading capacity of the complexes.
Following the preparation of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF@Cur and $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs@Cur inclusion complexes, interactions between the drug and complexes were evaluated using FTIR spectroscopy (Fig. 5A). The FTIR spectrum of Cur showed intense and well-defined bands at $3504\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (Phe–OH stretching vibration), $1625\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ( $\scriptstyle\mathtt{C}=0$ stretching vibration), $1429~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–H bending vibration), $1278~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–O stretching vibration) and $1025~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $_{(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O}-\mathsf{C}}$ stretching vibration) (Baby et al., 2021). By comparing the spectra of the inclusion com­ pounds, we found that Cur was encapsulated in the complexes by hydrogen bonding and hydrophobic interactions. In addition, the optical and thermal stability of Cur in the complexes were determined. The fluorescence spectrum of Cur dispersed in $70\%$ ethanol was similar to that of Cur in $\beta$ -CDMOF $@\mathrm{Cur}$ at an excitation wavelength of $425~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. S7), indicating that the interaction between Cur and $\beta$ -CDMOF crystals preserved the photo-physical properties of Cur. However, the spectrum of Cur encapsulated in SHPs hydrogel was found to be significantly blue shifted $(\sim32\mathrm{nm})$ compared to the spectrum of Cur in ethanol, which confirmed that a hydrophobic interaction occurred be­ tween Cur and SHPs. Similar blue shifts also occurred in the fluorescence spectra of SHPs@β-CDMOF $@\mathrm{Cur}$ $(\sim5~\mathrm{{nm}})$ and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@{\mathrm{SHPs}}@{\mathrm{Cur}}$ $(\sim8~\mathrm{nm})$ complexes. Evidence of the photo-physical stability of Cur in the visible region was obtained from UV–visible absorption spectra (Fig. 5B), in which the characteristic absorption peaks in the spectra all appeared near $430\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The DSC curves of the inclusion complexes demonstrated the thermal stability of Cur (Fig. 5C). An obvious endo­ thermic peak appeared at $177.5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the pure Cur curve, due to py­ rolysis of the drug. Two obvious endothermic peaks at 148.2 and $176.3\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were observed in the $\beta$ -CDMOF@Cur curve. The first endo­ thermic peak $(-148.2^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ was due to solvent volatilization in the MOF structure, while the second peak $_{,-176.3}^{}\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ was due to pyrolysis of Cur. Although a broad melting peak in the $S_{\mathrm{HPs}@\mathrm{Cur}}$ curve obscured the drug pyrolysis, it should be noted that the curves of $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -
CDMOF@Cur and $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs@Cur were similar to that of $\beta$ -CDMOF $@\mathrm{Cur}$ . Thus, the DSC data established that the stability of Cur was effectively protected in the complexes.
The in vitro release of drug in phosphate buffered saline was analyzed under different conditions (Bazban-Shotorbani et al., 2017). The amount of Cur released was quantified by measuring the absorption of the in­ cubation solution. The cumulative release profiles of Cur from inclusion complexes are presented in Fig. 6A. Overall, the relative arrangement of MOF and hydrogel resulted in different rates of drug release. In partic­ ular, $81.08\%$ Cur was released from $\beta$ -CDMOF@Cur within $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The amount of Cur released from $\beta{\cdot}\mathrm{CDMOF}@\mathrm{SHPs}@\mathrm{Cur}_{\cdot}$ , which had a den­ dritic sandwich structure obtained by the post-synthesis protocol, was $50.36\%$ within $24\mathrm{h}$ . However, $66.04\%$ of Cur was released in the same time from $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF@Cur, obtained by the in situ growth pro­ tocol, because the MOF crystals in the necklace structure were exposed to the outside. The release of Cur from $S_{\mathrm{HPs}@\mathrm{Cur}}$ was $27.06\%$ , which may be due to its lower loading capacity and interaction with drug. In addition, kinetic release models were employed to evaluate the in vitro drug release data. The values obtained by fitting the key parameters of the profiles are presented in Table 2. The best linearity was obtained using a first-order equation plot $(R^{2}=0.954$ and $\mathbf{k}=0.131$ ) indicating that drug release from $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ Cur conformed to dissolution of the porous matrix drug dosage form, which also matched the structure and properties of the MOF. The data from the $S_{\mathrm{HPs}@\mathrm{Cur}}$ release curve fitted well with the Korsmeyer-Peppas model following Fickian diffusion $(\boldsymbol{R}^{2}$ $=0.944$ , $\mathbf{k}=7.298$ and $n=0.438$ ) (Peppas & Sahlin, 1989; Ritger & Peppas, 1986; Ritger & Peppas, 1987). However, the release of Cur from $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF@Cur and $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs@Cur was by non-Fickian diffusion (abnormal transmission) and their fitting coefficients $(R^{2}=$ 0.925 and 0.991) and release exponents $\scriptstyle{\cdot}n=0.459$ and 0.524) indicated that drug release occurred by diffusion and matrix collapse. Finally, based on the above results, we believe that the complexes obtained from SHPs hydrogel and $\beta$ -CDMOF can exhibit different release behavior in the specific $\mathsf{p H}$ ranges of the gastrointestinal tract (Fig. 6B). The Cur in $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ Cur and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF@SHPs@Cur}}$ is released at a lower rate in $\mathtt{p H}2.0$ medium (empty stomach), which is mainly caused by passive dissolution of Cur (hydrophobic effect) while Cur encapsulated by hydrogen bonding in the complexes is retained in the structure. When the $\mathtt{p H}$ value is increased to 4.0 (fed stomach), Cur release from $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF@Cur is unchanged. Meanwhile, release of Cur from $\beta{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CDMOF}@\mathrm{SHPs}@\mathrm{Cur}$ decreases, mainly because the pH value of the medium is close to a value $(\sim4.5)$ where SHPs reaches ionization equilibrium. Under these conditions, the SHPs hydrogel structure shrinks and the gel network becomes more compact, preventing solvent exchange between the release medium and drug. When the $\mathsf{p H}$ is increased to 6.8 (colon), the release of Cur from $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF@Cur reaches a plateau due to disintegration of the MOF and dissolution of the hydrogel. However, Cur is released at a higher rate from $\beta$ -CDMOF@SHPs@Cur, indicating that the outer hydrogel of the den­ dritic sandwich had swollen and the inner MOF disintegrated, resulting in weakened hydrogen bonding and hydrophobicity between drug and complex, permitting dissolution of Cur. When the $\mathsf{p H}$ value was increased to 7.4 (small intestine), with the exception that the $\operatorname{SHPs}(\varnothing\beta.$ - CDMOF $@$ Cur release profile became flat, similar release mechanisms were observed for both complexes.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have developed two complexes containing hydrogel and MOF by using in situ growth and post-synthesis protocols. Based on these different strategies, a necklace-shaped ${\S}\mathrm{HPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF complex with MOF crystals and a dendritic sandwich-shaped $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ SHPs complex were obtained. Furthermore, interactions between $\beta$ -CDMOF and SHPs in the complexes were investigated, revealing that coordina­ tion plays an important role in the in situ growth of MOF around SHPs hydrogel in ${\S}\mathrm{HPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF. Coordination and hydrogen bonding are the main driving forces for the formation of dendritic sandwich $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@{\mathrm{SHPs}}$ . We also demonstrated the potential of $\operatorname{SHPs}(\varnothing\beta.$ - CDMOF and $\beta{\mathrm{-CDMOF}}@{\mathrm{SHPs}}$ complexes in the field of drug delivery. The release profiles and kinetic results obtained using a model drug demonstrated that release of Cur from $\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ SHPs@Cur and $\operatorname{SHPs}@\beta$ -CDMOF $@$ Cur conformed to non-Fickian diffusion. Active diffusion and passive release triggered by disintegration of the com­ plexes both contributed to the release of Cur. In the future, it will be helpful to develop advanced functional materials for specific needs by integrating the best properties of different materials. Coupling MOFs to polymers is one pathway to hybrid materials design and will enable the fabrication of composites to advance beyond powders in different forms with specific applications, such as drug delivery, food packaging, bio­ catalysis, separation, and gas storage, etc.
